From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 19:40:46 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] UNFILED 89 review lows: the scale's helper is counted; no
 session code in the test

- test_1089's operator case counts the calls to _sum_abs_deterministic (a
  monkeypatch). A _velocity_diag_scale rewired back to NORM_1 makes no such
  call and fails the test serially: 3 runs of 3, 0 calls against 30.
- The helper's docstring says it must read the values where they sit. A sum
  that copied first would put every offset at one alignment and pass the
  offset test while still depending on it.
- The test docstring describes the fixture (a rotated free-slip annulus with
  viscosity 1 + T, re-solved warm after T changes) instead of naming the
  session that measured it.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
---
 src/underworld3/utilities/rotated_bc.py       |  7 +++++-
 ...89_velocity_diag_scale_is_bit_identical.py | 22 ++++++++++++++-----
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/underworld3/utilities/rotated_bc.py b/src/underworld3/utilities/rotated_bc.py
index 7c9f5559..322b8aa5 100644
--- a/src/underworld3/utilities/rotated_bc.py
+++ b/src/underworld3/utilities/rotated_bc.py
@@ -2740,7 +2740,12 @@ def _sum_abs_deterministic(values):
     ``math.fsum`` rounds once, from the exact sum, so neither the summation order
     nor the array's alignment can move it. BLAS dasum (PETSc's ``NORM_1``) can:
     on AVX-512 OpenBLAS the same data at different offsets from a 64-byte boundary
-    gives one of two results an ulp apart."""
+    gives one of two results an ulp apart.
+
+    It must read ``values`` where they sit and never copy them into a fresh
+    buffer: test_1089 checks it at 8 offsets, and a sum that copied first (a
+    NORM_1 on a new Vec, say) would put every offset at the same alignment and
+    pass while still depending on it."""
     import math
 
     return math.fsum(np.abs(np.asarray(values)).tolist())
diff --git a/tests/test_1089_velocity_diag_scale_is_bit_identical.py b/tests/test_1089_velocity_diag_scale_is_bit_identical.py
index 416a17a3..9360d0ba 100644
--- a/tests/test_1089_velocity_diag_scale_is_bit_identical.py
+++ b/tests/test_1089_velocity_diag_scale_is_bit_identical.py
@@ -5,13 +5,15 @@ the eliminated wall-normal rows by ``zeroRowsColumns``) was ``NORM_1`` of a
 sub-vector, which is BLAS dasum. On AVX-512 OpenBLAS dasum of the same data gives
 one of two results an ulp apart depending on where the array sits in memory, and
 getSubVector's array lands somewhere new on each call. The ulp moved Krylov
-round-off: on H7's warm re-solve fixture (an annulus, viscosity 1 + T, T from 0 to
-1) the pressure sub-solve took 12, 13 or its 200 cap from run to run at np 2, with
-the same answer.
+round-off: on a rotated free-slip annulus with viscosity 1 + T, re-solved warm after
+T changes from 0 to 1, the pressure sub-solve took 12, 13 or its 200 cap from run
+to run at np 2, with the same answer.
 
 - the per-rank sum gives the same bits for the same data at every memory offset
   (dasum, measured on the review machine, does not);
-- the scale is the same on repeated calls and on freshly assembled operators.
+- the scale is the same on repeated calls and on freshly assembled operators, and
+  it is computed through that helper (so a scale rewired to NORM_1 fails here in
+  serial too).
 """
 import numpy as np
 import pytest
@@ -44,7 +46,7 @@ def test_the_per_rank_sum_does_not_depend_on_where_the_array_sits(n):
     assert len(sums) == 1, sorted(sums)
 
 
-def test_the_diagonal_scale_is_the_same_on_every_call_and_every_assembly():
